`@core/collections` forms the binding-backed Core package that provides runtime functions for compiler-owned collection methods. Authors do not bind `@core/collections` directly. The compiler lowers collection receiver methods to calls against this package.

`@core/collections` belongs to the Core origin package family with ExternalBinding backing. See @../../#package-origins-and-backing (Package origins and backing) for origin and backing rules.

### Binding-backed behaviour

The package registers five host functions that implement the compiler-owned collection member operations:

[table:
    [th:
        [: Host function]
        [: Compiler-owned member]
        [: Receiver access]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `__moth_collection_get`]
        [: `get(index)`]
        [: shared]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `__moth_collection_set`]
        [: `set(index, value)`]
        [: mutable]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `__moth_collection_push`]
        [: `push(value)`]
        [: mutable]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `__moth_collection_remove`]
        [: `remove(index)`]
        [: mutable]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `__moth_collection_length`]
        [: `length()`]
        [: shared]
    ]
]

These host functions use inferred ABI types for the collection element and value parameters. The index parameter is `I32`. The length return is `I32`. Fallible operations return through the builtin `Error!` channel.

### Source-visible surface

Authors interact with collections through language-level syntax and receiver methods, not through direct dependencies on `@core/collections`. The compiler-owned methods include:

- `get(index)` returns the element at `index` or fails with `Error!`
- `set(index, value)` replaces the element at `index` or fails with `Error!`
- `push(value)` appends `value` after the last element. On a growable `{T}` it is infallible; on a fixed `{N T}` it fails with `Error!` when the collection is full
- `remove(index)` removes and returns the element at `index` or fails with `Error!`
- `length()` returns the logical length as `Int`

`get`, `set`, `remove` and fixed-collection `push` take the fallible path. `length` and growable `push` stay infallible. `set`, `push` and `remove` require mutable receiver access through `~`.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    values ~= {1, 2, 3}

    ~values.push(4)

    first = values.get(0) catch:
        then 0
    ;

    ~values.set(1, 99) catch:
    ;

    count = values.length()
]

### Current implementation debt

The registered host functions above describe the **current** implementation, which still uses one unified fallible `__moth_collection_push` for both growable and fixed receivers. That does not yet match the accepted source contract, under which growable `push` is infallible and only fixed `push` is fallible.

Splitting the unified helper is owned by `docs/roadmap/plans/collection-push-fallibility-split-plan.md`. Until that plan executes, the compiler and runtime still treat every `push` as fallible. See @../../../progress (progress matrix) for the current-state row.

### No direct dependency

`@core/collections` carries no source-visible dependency surface. Do not write a dependency clause for it. The package exists to back the compiler-owned methods that receiver syntax invokes.

### Unsupported source forms

- Direct dependencies on `@core/collections` are not part of the author surface.
- Square-bracket indexing is not supported. Use `get` and `set`.
- Assignment through `get` was removed.
- Builtin member arguments are positional-only. Named arguments are not supported.

### Backend behaviour

HTML-JS lowers the host functions to JavaScript runtime helpers. HTML-Wasm lacks a Wasm lowering for these functions. See @../../../progress (progress matrix) for current target availability.

### Deferred surfaces

Deferred collection surfaces include HTML-Wasm lowering, map operations as direct host functions, collection equality, collection iteration protocol, sortable collections, deque operations, fixed-capacity collections and specialised collection variants. See @../../../progress (progress matrix) for current status.

### Related concepts

- For collection literal syntax and typing see @../../../collections (Collections).
- For the full collection method contract see @../../../collections/#collection-operations (Collection operations).
- For package origin and backing see @../../#package-origins-and-backing (Package origins and backing).
